    topic: 'A tech support call about a broken connection',
    audioText: "Dilnoza calls the help line of her internet provider at ten past nine on a Monday morning because her connection has been dropping for three days. The agent, a patient man called Martin, asks her to describe the problem. She explains that the wireless signal disappears every evening around seven, just when her children want to watch their cartoons. At first she says the router is the white box in the hall, but then she corrects herself and says it is actually the black one on the bookshelf, because the white box is only a telephone socket.\n\nMartin asks her to unplug the router for thirty seconds and plug it in again. Dilnoza complains that she has already done that twice and that nothing changed. Martin says he understands, but he must follow the checklist before he can send an engineer. While the router restarts, he asks which lights are on. She reports that the power light is green, the wireless light is green, and the internet light is orange. Martin explains that an orange internet light usually means the problem is outside the house, not inside it, so she does not need to buy a new router, which is exactly what her neighbour had advised her to do last week.\n\nNext Martin checks the line from his office and sees a fault on the cable in her street. He says an engineer could come on Wednesday between one and three in the afternoon. Dilnoza sighs, because she works on Wednesday afternoons, and she asks whether Thursday morning is possible. Martin looks at the calendar and says Thursday is completely full, but Friday morning between nine and eleven is still free. She decides that Friday will be fine, even though she would have preferred to have the problem solved sooner.\n\nBefore they finish, Martin warns her about the cost. The visit is free if the fault is in the street, but if the engineer finds that the problem is inside her flat, the call-out fee is fifty pounds. Dilnoza thought he had said fifteen, so he repeats the number slowly. She is not happy, but she accepts the risk, since the orange light makes her fairly sure the fault is outside. Finally, Martin apologises for the trouble and offers a credit of twelve pounds for the three days without a reliable service. He says it will appear on her next bill, and he gives her a reference number to write down in case she needs to call again. Dilnoza thanks him, although she says she will believe the repair when she actually sees it, because the company has let her down twice before.",
