    topic: 'A radio report on a new telescope',
    audioText: "Good morning, and welcome to the science bulletin. Our main story today comes from the Atacama Desert in Chile, where astronomers have finally switched on the Aurora Array, a telescope that has taken eleven years to build. The project was supposed to be ready last March, but a series of dust storms and a strike by truck drivers pushed the first observation back to October. Professor Aigerim Nurlanova, who leads the international team, told our reporter that she had almost given up hope of meeting even the new deadline.\n\nThe Aurora Array is not the biggest telescope in the world, a title that still belongs to a competing project in Hawaii, but its supporters argue that size is not everything. Instead of one enormous mirror, it uses fifty-five smaller mirrors that work together as a single eye. This design, according to the professor, makes the images sharper and the whole system cheaper to repair, because a damaged mirror can be replaced in a week without stopping the observations. The total cost was just under two billion dollars, which she admits is a great deal of money, although she insists it is less than the original estimate.\n\nThe first target of the telescope will be a star about fourteen light years from Earth, which is known to have at least two planets. The team hopes to find out whether either of them has an atmosphere containing water vapour. Professor Nurlanova was careful not to promise too much. She said that a result of that kind would take months of careful analysis, and that anyone expecting a sensational announcement by the end of the year would be disappointed.\n\nNot everyone is celebrating. Several families who live in the nearest village, about forty kilometres away, complain that the observatory uses a large amount of water in a region where rain is rare. A spokesman for the project replied that most of the water is recycled and that the observatory has paid for a new well that will also serve the village. Our reporter, however, noticed that several villagers were still not convinced, and one farmer said he would wait to see the well working before he changed his mind.\n\nDespite these doubts, the mood at the site on opening night was cheerful. About two hundred guests watched the first image appear on a screen, and when it did, the professor, who rarely shows her feelings, wiped her eyes. The first public pictures will be released next Friday. And now, the weather.",
