    topic: 'A conversation in a phone shop',
    audioText: "Bobur walks into a phone shop on Saturday afternoon because his old phone, which he has had for four years, keeps switching itself off. A young assistant called Malika greets him and asks what he is looking for. Bobur says he wants something reliable with a good camera, and that he does not want to spend more than four hundred dollars. Malika nods and shows him two models. The first costs three hundred and eighty dollars and has a very good battery. The second costs four hundred and fifty, has a better camera, and is thinner and lighter.\n\nBobur picks up the second one and says that he really likes it, but it is over his budget. Malika says she understands, but she reminds him that the shop gives a discount of fifty dollars if a customer brings an old phone to trade in. Bobur laughs and says that his phone is hardly worth fifty cents. Malika checks it and, to his surprise, says it is worth thirty dollars, because the screen is still in good condition. That would bring the price of the second phone down to four hundred and twenty dollars, which is slightly more than he planned to spend. Bobur thinks for a moment and decides that for a better camera he can accept it.\n\nThen Malika asks how much storage he needs. The cheaper version has sixty-four gigabytes, and the more expensive one has two hundred and fifty-six. Bobur says he takes a lot of photographs of his daughter and asks whether sixty-four is enough. Malika answers honestly that it will probably fill up within a year, and she suggests buying a memory card instead. Unfortunately, this model does not take a card. So Bobur has to choose the larger version, which costs another sixty dollars. He sighs and says that the bill keeps growing, but he agrees.\n\nMalika also mentions a case and a screen protector. She explains that the shop sells them as a set for twenty dollars, which is cheaper than buying them separately. Bobur says no at first, because he feels he has already spent enough, but she points out that a repair would cost more than ten times as much, and he changes his mind. She tells him the phone will be ready in twenty minutes, as she has to move his photographs from the old one. Meanwhile, Bobur can have a free coffee at the café next door. He thanks her, and as he leaves he says that he has never had such a helpful shop assistant. Malika smiles and says that he should tell her manager.",
