    topic: 'Judging a school science fair',
    audioText: "It is Thursday, the day of the school science fair, and Ms Ibragimova, the physics teacher who organised it, is speaking to the judges before the doors open. Thank you all for coming, she says. This year we have forty-two projects, which is a record, and I am afraid the weather has caused us a small problem. We planned to show the solar projects in the yard, but the forecast is for rain all afternoon, so everything will be indoors in the sports hall. The judges will therefore start at ten, not at nine as it says on your timetable, because the caretaker needs an extra hour to arrange the tables.\n\nShe then describes the projects the judges should look for. The first is by a boy named Temur, who built a solar oven out of a pizza box and aluminium foil. He claims that it can bake a biscuit in forty minutes on a sunny day, although his experiment last week gave a result of fifty minutes, so please ask him about the difference. The second is by two girls, Aisha and Nora, who tested whether plants grow faster when they listen to music. Ms Ibragimova admits that she was sceptical at first, because the idea sounds more like a joke than a science project. However, the girls kept careful records for six weeks, and she was impressed. Their result was that the plants with classical music grew slightly taller, but the difference was so small that it could be an accident, and, to their credit, they say so in their report.\n\nThe third project is a water filter made by Jamshid from sand, stones and charcoal. It cleans muddy water well enough to see through, but he does not claim that it is safe to drink, and the judges should not drink it either. Ms Ibragimova adds that, of all the projects, this one is probably the most useful in practice, and she would like the judges to look at it carefully.\n\nNext, the rules for judging. Each judge gives marks out of ten for three things: the idea, the quality of the experiment, and the explanation. Marks for how the project looks should not count, since a plain board with a good experiment is better than a colourful one without. The winner will receive a prize of one hundred pounds for the school's science club, and second and third places will receive books. The results will be announced at half past three in the main hall, and parents are invited.\n\nFinally, she thanks everyone and says that she hopes the judges will be kind, because some of the students have not slept for two nights. The doors open at ten, and the first group of visitors is already waiting outside in the rain.",
